"CSS Cascading and Inheritance Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/)\nciteturn1317view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1315view1\",\"pattern\":\"actual value\"}); Total lines: 1419\nL277:   4. Resolving value dependencies yields the cite203†computed value . Every element has exactly one computed value per property.\nL278:   5. Formatting the document yields the cite204†used value . An element only has a used value for a given property if that property applies to the element.\nL279:   6. Finally, the used value is transformed to the cite205†actual value based on constraints of the display environment. As with the cite204†used value , there may or may not be an actual value for a given property on an element.\nL331: A cite204†used value is in principle ready to be used, but a user agent may not be able to make use of the value in a given environment. For example, a user agent may only be able to render borders with integer pixel widths and may therefore have to approximate the cite204†used width. Also, the font size of an element may need adjustment based on the availability of fonts or the value of the cite236†font-size-adjust property. The actual value is the used value after any such adjustments have been made.\nL332: Note: By probing the actual values of elements, much can be learned about how the document is laid out. However, not all information is recorded in the actual values. For example, the actual value of the cite237†page-break-after property does not reflect whether there is a page break or not after the element. Similarly, the actual value of cite238†orphans does not reflect how many orphan lines there is in a certain element. See examples (j) and (k) in the cite46†table below .\nL333: ### 4.7. Examples\nL334: Examples of CSS Value Computation\nL335:  | Property  | Winning declaration  | Cascaded value  | Specified value  | Computed value  | Used value  | Actual value\nL336: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL337: (a)  | cite239†text-align | `text-align: left`  | left  | left  | left  | left  | left\nL338: (b)  | cite240†border-top-width , cite241†border-right-width , cite242†border-bottom-width , cite243†border-left-width | `border-width: inherit`  | inherit  | 4.2px  | 4.2px  | 4.2px  | 4px\nL339: (c)  | cite218†width | (none)  | (none)  | auto (initial value)  | auto  | 120px  | 120px\nL340: (d)  | cite244†list-style-position | `list-style-position: inherit`  | inherit  | inside  | inside  | inside  | inside\nL341: (e)  | cite244†list-style-position | `list-style-position: initial`  | initial  | outside (initial value)  | outside  | outside  | outside\nL342: (f)  | cite104†font-size | `font-size: 1.2em`  | 1.2em  | 1.2em  | 14.1px  | 14.1px  | 14px\nL343: (g)  | cite218†width | `width: 80%`  | 80%  | 80%  | 80%  | 354.2px  | 354px\nL344: (h)  | cite218†width | `width: auto`  | auto  | auto  | auto  | 134px  | 134px\nL345: (i)  | cite245†height | `height: auto`  | auto  | auto  | auto  | 176px  | 176px\nL346: (j)  | cite237†page-break-after | (none)  | (none)  | auto (initial value)  | auto  | auto  | auto\nL347: (k)  | cite238†orphans | `orphans: 3`  | 3  | 3  | 3  | 3  | 3\nL1031:   * cite205†actual , in § 4.6\nL1032:   * cite205†actual value , in § 4.6\nL1033:   * cite184†all , in § 3.2\nL1034:   * cite309†Animation Origin , in § 6.2\nL1035:   * cite217†apply to , in § 4.5.1\nL1036:   * cite261†author origin , in § 6.2\nL1037:   * cite261†author-origin , in § 6.2\nL1038:   * cite295†author presentational hint origin , in § 6.5\nL1039:   * cite261†author style sheet , in § 6.2\nL1040:   * cite111†cascade , in § 6\nL1041:   * cite202†cascaded , in § 4.2\nL1042:   * cite350†cascade-dependent keyword , in § 7.3\nL1043:   * cite202†cascaded value , in § 4.2\nL1044:   * cite21†cascade layers , in § 6.4\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Box Sizing Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-sizing-3/)\nciteturn1317view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1315view2\",\"pattern\":\"cyclic\"}); Total lines: 1331\nL816: Sometimes the size of a percentage-sized box’s cite139†containing block depends on the cite1033†intrinsic size contribution of the box itself, creating a cyclic dependency. When calculating the intrinsic size contribution of such a box (including any calculations for a content-based cite1034†automatic minimum size ), a percentage value that resolves against a size in the same axis as the intrinsic size contribution (a cyclic percentage size) is resolved specially:\nL817:   1. If the box is cite202†non-replaced , then the entire value of any cite37†max size property or cite35†preferred size property (cite70†width /cite111†max-width /cite71†height /cite112†max-height ) specified as an expression cite1035†containing a percentage (such as 10% or calc(10px + 0%)) that is cite1036†cyclic is treated for the purpose of calculating the box’s cite1033†intrinsic size contributions only as that property’s cite1037†initial value .\nL818: For example, given a box with width: calc(20px + 50%), its max-content contribution is calculated as if its width were cite160†auto . (The percentage is honored as usual, however, during the actual sizing of the box itself; see below.)\nL819:   2. Likewise, if the box is cite174†replaced , then the entire value of any cite37†max size property or cite35†preferred size property specified as an expression containing a percentage that is cite1036†cyclic is treated for the purpose of calculating the box’s cite182†max-content contributions only as that property’s cite1037†initial value .\nL820:   3. If the box is cite174†replaced , a cite1036†cyclic percentage in the value of any cite37†max size property or cite35†preferred size property (cite70†width /cite111†max-width /cite71†height /cite112†max-height ), is resolved against zero when calculating the cite183†min-content contribution in the corresponding axis.\nL821: (See cite50†§ 5.2.2 Compressible Replaced Elements for a list of which elements in HTML this applies to.) If the box also has a cite184†preferred aspect ratio , then this min-content contribution is floored by any cite207†<length-percentage> cite179†minimum size from the opposite axis—​resolving any such percentage against zero—​transferred through the preferred aspect ratio.\nL822: Should we resolve transferred percentages against their containing block instead of zero before transferring them? See cite1038†discussion†github.com .\nL823: \nL824: The UA may additionally floor the cite183†min-content contribution based on UI considerations, such as ensuring certain UI elements remain visible (for example, the dropdown arrow on a `cite1039†select†html.spec.whatwg.org `).\nL825: \nL826: Note: The cite183†min-content contribution is, as always, also floored by the cite179†minimum size in its own axis.\nL827: This rule also applies when calculating a content-based cite1034†automatic minimum size or its corresponding size contribution, yielding a cite138†definite “specified size suggestion”.\nL828: \nL829: Tests\nL830:      * cite1040†intrinsic-percent-replaced-028.html†wpt.fyi cite1041†(live test)†wpt.live cite1042†(source)†github.com L831: \nL832: For example, an `cite857†input†html.spec.whatwg.org ` assigned cite70†width: calc(50% + 50px) has a cite183†min-content contribution of 50px, plus any horizontal margin/border/padding.\nL833:   4. For the cite36†min size properties , as well as for cite629†margins and cite627†paddings (and cite1043†gutters ), a cite1036†cyclic percentage is resolved against zero for determining cite1033†intrinsic size contributions .\nL834: Tests\nL835:   * cite1044†inline-intrinsic-size-calc.html†wpt.fyi cite1045†(live test)†wpt.live cite1046†(source)†github.com L836:   * cite1047†intrinsic-percent-non-replaced-006.html†wpt.fyi cite1048†(live test)†wpt.live cite1049†(source)†github.com L837: Summary of the Cyclic-Percentage Intrinsic Size Contribution Rules (Above)\nL838: Element Type  | cite174†Replaced | cite202†Non-replaced L839: --- | --- | ---\nL840: Contribution Type  | cite183†min-content | cite182†max-content | cite183†min-content | cite182†max-content L841: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL842: cite36†min size & cite1050†margin /cite1051†padding | cite1052†zeroᵈ | cite1052†zeroᵈ | cite1052†zeroᵈ | cite1052†zeroᵈ L843: cite37†max & cite35†preferred size | cite1053†zeroᶜ | cite1054†initialᵇ | cite1055†initialᵃ | cite1055†initialᵃ L844: Then, unless otherwise specified, when calculating the used sizes and positions of the containing block’s contents:\nL845: \nL846:   * If the cyclic dependency was introduced due to a cite166†block-axis size other than a cite179†minimum size on the containing block (i.e. a cite193†block-size or cite284†max-block-size in most layout modes, or a cite579†flex-basis in cite801†flex layout ) that causes it to depend on the size of its contents, the box’s percentage is not resolved and instead cite581†behaves as auto .\nL847: Note: cite1056†Grid items in both axes, cite1057†flex items in the cite1058†main axis , and children of flex items in both axes do allow percentages to resolve in this case.\nL848: \nL849:   * Otherwise, the percentage is resolved against the containing block’s size. (The containing block’s size is not re-resolved based on the resulting size of the box; the contents might thus overflow or underflow the containing block).\nL850: Note: These rules specify the previously-undefined behavior of this cyclic case in cite1059†CSS2§10.2†www.w3.org , cite1060†CSS2§8.3†www.w3.org , and cite1061†CSS2§8.4†www.w3.org . Note also, the behavior in cite580†CSS2§10.5†www.w3.org is superseded in their respective specifications for layout modes (such as cite1062†flex layout†www.w3.org ) not described in CSS2.\nL851: For example, in the following markup:\nL852: \nL853:     <article style=\"width: min-content\">\nL854:       <aside style=\"width: 50%;\">\nL855:       LOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOONG\nL856:       </aside>\nL857:     </article>\nL858: When calculating the width of the outer `<article>`, the inner `<aside>` behaves as cite70†width: auto , so the `<article>` sets itself to the width of the long word. Since the `<article>`’s width didn’t depend on \"real\" layout, though, it’s treated as cite138†definite for resolving the `<aside>`, whose width resolves to half that of the `<article>`.\nL1027:   * cite656†border-box , in § 3.3\nL1028:   * cite199†<box-size> , in § 3.2\nL1029:   * cite115†box-sizing , in § 3.3\nL1030:   * cite1119†content-box , in § 3.3\nL1031:   * cite1036†cyclic percentage , in § 5.2.1\nL1032:   * cite1036†cyclic percentage size , in § 5.2.1\nL1033:   * cite138†definite , in § 2\nL1034:   * cite138†definite size , in § 2\nL1035:   * cite1120†Extrinsic sizing , in § 4\nL1036:   * cite161†fallback , in § 2\nL1037:   * cite161†fallback size , in § 2\nL1038:   * fit-content\nL1039:     * cite668†value for column-width , in § 3.4\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Text Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text-3/)\nciteturn1317view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1316view1\",\"pattern\":\"exact set\"}); Total lines: 4797\nNo matching text found for \"exact set\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/)\nciteturn1317view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1316view2\",\"pattern\":\"implementation-defined\"}); Total lines: 2959\nL526: The precision and supported range of numeric values in CSS is cite287†implementation-defined†infra.spec.whatwg.org , and can vary based on the property or other context a value is used in. However, within the CSS specifications, infinite precision and range is assumed. When a value cannot be explicitly supported due to range/precision limitations, it must be converted to the closest value supported by the implementation, but how the implementation defines \"closest\" is implementation-defined as well.\nL527: If an cite281†<angle> must be converted due to exceeding the implementation-defined range of supported values, it must be clamped to the nearest supported multiple of 360deg.\nL528: ### 5.1. Range Restrictions and Range Definition Notation\nL529: Properties can restrict numeric values to some range. If the value is outside the allowed range, then unless otherwise specified, the declaration is invalid and must be cite288†ignored†www.w3.org . Range restrictions can be annotated in the numeric type notation using CSS bracketed range notation—​`[`min`,`max`]`—​within the angle brackets, after the identifying keyword, indicating a closed range between (and including) `min` and `max`.\nL530: For example, cite147†<integer [0,10]> indicates an integer between 0 and 10, inclusive, while cite281†<angle [0,180deg]> indicates an angle between 0deg and 180deg (expressed in any unit).\nL531: Note: CSS values generally do not allow open ranges; thus only square-bracket notation is used.\nL532: \nL533: CSS theoretically supports infinite precision and infinite ranges for all value types; however in reality implementations have finite capacity. cite158†UAs should support reasonably useful ranges and precisions. Range extremes that are ideally unlimited are indicated using ∞ or −∞ as appropriate. For example, cite127†<length [0,∞]> indicates a non-negative length.\nL714: While the exact supported precision of numeric values, and how they are rounded to match that precision, is generally cite287†implementation-defined†infra.spec.whatwg.org , cite127†<length> s in cite142†border-width and a few other properties are rounded in a specific fashion to ensure reasonable visual display. (This algorithm is called by individual properties explicitly.)\nL715: \nL716: To snap a length as a line width given a cite127†<length> `len`:\nL717: \nL718:   1. If `len` is an integer number of cite390†device pixels , do nothing.\nL854: Some user-agents apply restrictions to the cite355†line-height values on form controls. These must have no effect on the cite397†lh and cite398†rlh units. The effect on their descendants, however, is cite287†implementation-defined†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1408: #### 10.4.1. Argument Ranges\nL1409: \nL1410: In sin(A), cos(A), or tan(A), if A is infinite, the result is NaN. (See cite96†§ 10.9 Type Checking for details on how cite181†math functions handle NaN.)\nL1411: \nL1412: In sin(A) or tan(A), if A is 0⁻, the result is 0⁻.\nL1413: In tan(A), if A is one of the asymptote values (such as 90deg, 270deg, etc), the numeric result is cite287†implementation-defined†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1414: If an implementation is capable of exactly representing these inputs, it should return +∞ for the asymptotes at `90deg + N*360deg`, and −∞ for the asymptotes at `-90deg + N*360deg`, but implementations are not required to be able to exactly represent these inputs (and if they can’t, will return whatever the correct numeric answer is for the closest approximation to the input they are capable of representing). Authors must not rely on cite750†tan() returning any particular value for these inputs.\nL1415: Why are these cite287†implementation-defined†infra.spec.whatwg.org ?\nL1416: \nL1417: The tangent function is discontinuous at its asymptotes: it approaches infinity from one side and negative infinity from the other side, and isn’t defined at the exact values of the asymptote.\nL1418: Further, whether or not the asymptotic values are exactly representable in implementations depends on how they internally store and manipulate angles; when written in degrees the values are simple (90deg, etc), but in radians the values are transcendental (pi / 2, etc) and cannot be exactly represented.\nL1419: So, even defining a specific behavior for these values is difficult; if an implementation uses radians internally, it would have to do some fuzzy matching to return the defined value when the input is sufficiently close to the asymptote.\nL1420: The other major language for the Web, JavaScript, exposes these functions as taking radians only, so it can’t hit the exact asymptotes either (and this true for most other computer languages, too). Authors writing code in JS, then, can’t rely on any specific behavior for these values either, and it’s unlikely that their needs in CSS are significantly different.\nL1546: #### 10.7.2. Degenerate Numeric Constants: cite768†infinity , cite769†-infinity , cite770†NaN L1547: \nL1548: When a cite715†calculation or a subtree of a calculation becomes cite771†infinite or cite772†NaN , representing it with a numeric value is no longer possible. A UA may have an implementation-defined limit for values approaching infinity.\nL1549: \nL1550: To aid in serialization of these degenerate values, the following additional math constants are defined:\nL1551: \nL1552: infinity\nL1553: \nL1554: \nL1555: the value positive infinity (+∞)\nL1556: \nL1557: -infinity\nL2411:   * cite676†Hz , in § 7.3\nL2412:   * cite395†ic , in § 6.1.1\nL2413:   * cite395†ic unit , in § 6.1.1\nL2414:   * cite189†<ident> , in § 4\nL2415:   * cite135†ident , in § 4\nL2416:   * cite135†identifier , in § 4\nL2417:   * cite968†implementation-defined limit for values approaching infinity , in § 10.7.2\nL2418:   * cite330†in , in § 6.2\nL2419:   * cite771†infinite , in § 10.9.2\nL2420:   * cite769†-infinity , in § 10.7.2\nL2421:   * infinity\nL2422:     * cite771†dfn for CSS , in § 10.9.2\nL2423:     * cite768†value for calc() , in § 10.7.2\nL2424:   * cite147†<integer> , in § 5.2\nL2425:   * cite320†integer , in § 5.2\nL2426:   * cite178†interpolate , in § 3\nL2790:     * code point\nL2791:     * concatenate\nL2792:     * for each\nL2793:     * identical to\nL2794:     * implementation-defined\nL2795:     * string\nL2796:   * [MATHML-CORE] defines the following terms:\nL2797:     * math-depth\nL2798:     * math-style\nL2799:   * [MEDIAQUERIES-5] defines the following terms:\nL2800:     * continuous media\nL2801:     * media query\nL2802:     * paged media\nL2803:   * [URL] defines the following terms:\nL2804:     * fragment\nL2805:     * percent-encode after encoding\nL2806:     * URL\nL2807:     * URL parser\nL2808:   * [WEB-ANIMATIONS-1] defines the following terms:\nL2809:     * discrete\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Conditional Rules Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-conditional-5/)\nciteturn1317view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1316view3\",\"pattern\":\"cycle\"}); Total lines: 1578\nNo matching text found for \"cycle\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn1317view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1315view3\",\"pattern\":\"underlying value\"}); Total lines: 4503\nL1830: #### 5.3.4. The effect value of a keyframe effect\nL1831: \nL1832: The cite378†effect value of a single property referenced by a cite237†keyframe effect as one of its cite186†target properties , for a given `iteration progress`, `iteration index` and `underlying value` is calculated as follows.\nL1833: \nL1834:   1. If `iteration progress` is cite180†unresolved abort this procedure.\nL1835: \nL1836:   2. Let `target property` be the cite410†longhand property for which the cite378†effect value is to be calculated.\nL1837:   3. If cite358†animation type of the `target property` is cite359†not animatable abort this procedure since the effect cannot be applied.\nL1838: \nL1839:   4. If the cite237†keyframe effect does not have an cite312†effect target , or if the effect target cannot have computed property values calculated, abort this procedure.\nL1840: \nL1841:   5. Let `property-specific keyframes` be the result of getting the set of cite402†computed keyframes for this cite237†keyframe effect .\nL1842:   6. Remove any cite379†keyframes from `property-specific keyframes` that do not have a property value for `target property`.\nL1843: \nL1844:   7. If `property-specific keyframes` is empty, return `underlying value`.\nL1845:   8. If there is no cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 0, create a new keyframe with a computed keyframe offset of 0, a property value set to `underlying value`, a cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation of cite421†replace , and a cite340†keyframe-specific easing function of the cite339†linear easing function , and prepend it to the beginning of `property-specific keyframes`.\nL1846:   9. Similarly, if there is no cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 1, create a new keyframe with a computed keyframe offset of 1, a property value set to `underlying value`, a cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation of cite421†replace , and a cite340†keyframe-specific easing function of the cite339†linear easing function , and append it to the end of `property-specific keyframes`.\nL1847: Other specifications may override the implicit keyframe generation behavior in the preceding two steps. For example, the cite422†named timeline range keyframe selectors defined by Scroll Animations can cause these implicit keyframes not to be generated.\nL1848: \nL1849: A future level of this specification should incorporate the overridden behavior directly into this algorithm.\nL1850: \nL1851:   1. Let `interval endpoints` be an empty sequence of keyframes.\nL1873:       3. Replace the property value of `target property` on `keyframe` with the result of combining `underlying value` (`V`_{a}) and `value to combine` (`V`_{b}) using the procedure for the `composite operation to use` corresponding to the `target property`’s cite358†animation type .\nL1874: \nL1875:   4. If there is only one keyframe in `interval endpoints` return the property value of `target property` on that keyframe.\nL1940:     1. If `A` and `B`’s cite288†associated animations differ by cite226†class , sort by any inter-class composite order as defined for the corresponding classes.\nL1941: \nL1942:     2. If `A` and `B` are still not sorted, sort by any cite226†class -specific composite order defined by the common class of `A` and `B`’s cite288†associated animations .\nL1943: \nL1944:     3. If `A` and `B` are still not sorted, sort by the position of their cite288†associated animations in the cite447†global animation list .\nL1945: cite174†Animation effects that sort earlier have lower composite order.\nL1946: #### 5.4.3. Calculating the result of an effect stack\nL1947: \nL1948: In order to calculate the final value of an cite355†effect stack , the cite378†effect values of each cite237†keyframe effect in the stack are combined in composite order.\nL1949: \nL1950: Each step in the process of evaluating an cite355†effect stack takes an underlying value as input.\nL1951: For each cite237†keyframe effect in the stack, the appropriate cite378†effect value from the keyframe effect is combined with the cite356†underlying value to produce a new value. This resulting value becomes the underlying value for combining the next keyframe effect in the stack.\nL1952: The final value of an cite355†effect stack , called the composited value, is simply the result of combining the cite378†effect value of the final (highest composite order) cite237†keyframe effect in the stack with the cite356†underlying value at that point.\nL1953: #### 5.4.4. Effect composition\nL1954: \nL1955: The specific operation used to combine an cite378†effect value with ancite356†underlying value is determined by the composite operation of the cite237†keyframe effect that produced the effect value.\nL1956: \nL1957: This specification defines three cite381†composite operations as follows:\nL1958: \nL1959: replace\nL1960: \nL1961: \nL1962: The result of compositing the cite378†effect value with the cite356†underlying value is simply the effect value.\nL1963: \nL1964: add\nL1965: The cite378†effect value is cite448†added to the cite356†underlying value . For cite358†animation types where the addition operation is defined such that it is not commutative, the order of the operands is `cite356†underlying value + cite378†effect value `.\nL1966: \nL1967: accumulate\nL1968: The cite378†effect value is cite449†accumulated onto the cite356†underlying value . For cite358†animation types where the accumulation operation is defined such that it is not commutative, the order of the operands is underlying value followed by effect value.\nL1969: \nL1970: Tests\nL1971: \nL1972:   * cite450†effect-composition.html†wpt.fyi cite451†(live test)†wpt.live cite452†(source)†github.com L1973: #### 5.4.5. Applying the composited result\nL1974: \nL1975: Applying a cite453†composited value to a cite186†target property is achieved by adding a specified value to the CSS cascade.\nL1976: \nL1977: The level of the cascade to which this specified value is added depends on the cite226†class of the cite173†animation cite288†associated with the effect with the highest composite order in the cite355†effect stack for a given property. By default, the specified value is added to the cite454†Animation Origin of the cascade. cite324†[CSS-CASCADE-3] L1978: \nL1979: Tests\nL1980:   * cite455†applying-the-composited-result.html†wpt.fyi cite456†(live test)†wpt.live cite457†(source)†github.com L1981: \nL1982: For example, if the effect with the highest composite order is associated with a “CSS transition”-class animation, the cite453†composited value will be added to the cite458†Transition Origin of the cascade.\nL1983: \nL1984: The cite453†composited value calculated for a CSS cite186†target property is applied using the following process:\nL1985:   1. Calculate the `base value` of the property as the value generated for that property by finding the cite362†computed value for that property in the absence of animations.\nL1986: \nL1987:   2. Establish the cite355†effect stack for the property (see cite106†§ 5.4.2 The effect stack ).\nL1988: \nL1989:   3. Calculate the cite453†composited value of the cite355†effect stack passing in the `base value` of the property as the initial cite356†underlying value (see cite107†§ 5.4.3 Calculating the result of an effect stack ).\nL1990:   4. Insert the cite453†composited value into the CSS cascade at the level defined for the cite226†class of the cite173†animation cite288†associated with the effect at the top of the cite355†effect stack established for the cite186†target property .\nL1991: ### 5.5. Replacing animations\nL1992: \nL1993: This section is non-normative\nL1994: \nL1995: Using the programming interface defined in this specification, it is possible to repeatedly trigger new animations that contribute to an element’s animated style indefinitely.\nL1996: \nL1997: For example, consider the following code:\nL1998: \nL1999:     elem.addEventListener('mousemove', evt => {\nL2000:       circle.animate(\nL2001:         { transform: `translate(${evt.clientX}px, ${evt.clientY}px)` },\nL2002:         { duration: 500, fill: 'forwards' }\nL2003:       );\nL2004:     });\nL3256: ### 6.7. The `cite636†CompositeOperation ` and `cite651†CompositeOperationOrAuto ` enumerations\nL3257: \nL3258: The possible values of an cite237†keyframe effect ’s composition behavior are represented by the `cite636†CompositeOperation ` enumeration.\nL3259: \nL3260:     enum `CompositeOperation` { cite690†\"replace\" , cite691†\"add\" , cite692†\"accumulate\" };\nL3261: \nL3262: \nL3263: `replace`\nL3264: \nL3265: \nL3266: Corresponds to the cite421†replace cite381†composite operation value such that the cite174†animation effect overrides the cite356†underlying value it is combined with.\nL3267: \nL3268: `add`\nL3269: Corresponds to the cite693†add cite381†composite operation value such that the cite174†animation effect is cite448†added to the cite356†underlying value with which it is combined.\nL3270: \nL3271: `accumulate`\nL3272: \nL3273: \nL3274: Corresponds to the cite694†accumulate cite381†composite operation value such that the cite174†animation effect is cite449†accumulated onto the cite356†underlying value .\n"
